# Actual selected-repository entry

Import package scheduling and call its additive exported function:

    func RankWorkspaceQueueExecutorWithin(
        ctx context.Context, q transaction.Executor,
        tenant string, entries []QueueEntry,
    ) ([]QueueEntry, error)

There is no constructor, authentication replacement or second pool.
QueueEntry has JobID and QueueID strings, Priority int32 and Sequence uint64.
QueueID is the Workspace ID. Tenant, job identities, priority and positive
durable enqueue sequence come from the workload owner's persisted authority.

The owner opens the selected Store.Within(ctx, true, callback). Its first SQL
statement is SET TRANSACTION ISOLATION LEVEL REPEATABLE READ, before reading
authority, the complete eligible batch or any probe. Through that same borrowed
executor it reads the batch, calls the ranker and resolves its candidate.
A bounded context and transaction completion remain with the owner.
Any callback or commit error publishes no selected candidate. Ranking itself
does not reserve, commit, open connections or change transaction mode.

Keep the old RankWorkspaceQueueWithin(ctx, pgx.Tx, tenant, entries) compatible.
Both entries now use identical mode, scope, policy, aggregate and rank logic.
The new helper rejects a Store/pool, missing context/executor, another engine,
read-only READ COMMITTED, writable REPEATABLE READ and read-only SERIALIZABLE.
ErrQueueSnapshotRequired describes invalid transaction setup. Existing quota,
scope, queue-validation and normalized storage errors propagate from the same
legacy implementation; this introduces no new HTTP error contract.

Read every competing eligible row in the same authorized tenant/local scope;
an HTTP page, client priority or caller timestamp is not an eligible batch.
The owner retains priority resolution, bounds/overflow refusal, requester and
Workspace reconciliation, and a fresh current-authority write admission.
No batch reader, training method, production queue or admission activation is
supplied by this packet. The separately retained ReserveQueuedWithin candidate
is neither copied nor required to consume this read entry.

The reader needs only existing SELECT grants for quota/reservations plus the
owner's eligible rows. Fixtures use an isolated superuser and do not qualify
serving-role grants or production readiness. ARC/IAM/workload owners retain
catalog, role, authenticated flow and installed acceptance responsibilities.
